With a slightly esoteric-looking setup (RDP from machine A to machine B=20
because A didn't have a serial port, serial connection from B to Alpha=20
for SRM console, Debian installation on Alpha's VGA console), I found=20
out that it was a crash in the PALcode. The machine worked after=20
removing the PCI SCSI RAID controller (Mylex DAC960, as far as I=20
remember correctly), getting as far as the hard drive detection and then=20
aborting because I didn't have any disks installed. :-D

I'll have to toy with the integrated disk stack. As far as I can tell,=20
you can connect it to the on-board SCSI controller either as one=20
probably-RAID-0-array (with up to eight disks) or two such arrays (four=20
disks each, probably a RAID 1/0). The current mode (as the box came from=20
the General Hospital in Vienna/AUT) is two-arrays where the first=20
would-be array had no disks and the second array was connected to the=20
Mylex. I'll have to replace two elements on the disk stack's PCB (a=20
terminator and a jumper) to switch it to one-array mode, which I'll=20
probably do tomorrow (if time and toolset allow).

I'll get rid of all PCI RAIDs since I absolutely don't feel like having=20
to shove them up an x86, reconfigure crap, put it back into the Lynx and=20
wait for it to fail for some esoteric reason. I wonder how all the=20
patients survived with THAT piece of hardware governing the storage. :-D

Thanks again for your patience. Once the system is up and running, I'll=20
drop a line so that everybody here knows Debian Testing works with Lynx.

~~ Ondra



